From: pocknix <pocknix@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/panel: il97680a-rpnova: expose only the 120Hz mode

Port of 1021 to the Retroid Pocket Nova panel, which has the same shape.

il97680a_rpnova_init_sequence() programs the panel's frame-rate register
(0x60: 0x00 for 120Hz, 0x01 for 60Hz) from il97680a_get_current_mode(),
which guesses the active mode from connector->state->crtc at prepare()
time - before the new state is reliably committed. With two modes exposed
the guess races on every panel enable, so the panel can be left programmed
for 60Hz while the DPU drives 120Hz timings.

The 60Hz entry is synthetic: identical timings to the 120Hz mode with only
the pixel clock halved. It is not reachable in practice either, since
gamescope refresh switching requires an EDID this DSI panel does not
provide. Expose only 120Hz so get_current_mode() has exactly one answer
and panel programming is deterministic on every enable.

Note the Nova panel is a video-mode panel (MIPI_DSI_MODE_VIDEO), not
command mode like the RP6, so it does not show the RP6's TE/tearcheck
CTL_START timeouts. The mis-programming race is the same; the symptom is
not.

Proper fix upstream would be plumbing the committed mode into prepare()
instead of guessing.

---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-ilitek-il97680a.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-ilitek-il97680a.c
@@ -192,7 +192,12 @@

 static struct panel_desc il97680a_rpnova_desc = {
 	.modes = il97680a_rpnova_modes,
-	.num_modes = ARRAY_SIZE(il97680a_rpnova_modes),
+	/*
+	 * 120Hz only: init_sequence guesses the active mode at prepare() time
+	 * and races when more than one mode is exposed (60Hz frame-rate
+	 * register vs 120Hz DPU timings). See 1021/1022.
+	 */
+	.num_modes = 1,
 	.width_mm = 91,
 	.height_mm = 68,
 	.bpc = 8,
